{"id":"h159","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Predict the number of lines in the EPR spectrum of a solution of dimethylnitroxide (CH3)2NO• assuming the lines do not overlap.\nIs the correct answer \"3\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct number of lines is 21, not 3.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The 14N nucleus (I=1) splits the spectrum into 3 lines.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Counting only nitrogen splitting gives 3 lines, which is what a naive count gives if protons are ignored.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The methyl protons also couple to the unpaired electron.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The two CH3 groups contain six equivalent spin-1/2 protons.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Six equivalent spin-1/2 nuclei give 7 lines.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nitrogen and proton couplings are independent, so the total line count is the product 3 x 7 = 21.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The proton hyperfine coupling cannot be ignored when counting lines in the total spectrum.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The value 3 accounts only for nitrogen and is therefore incorrect.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":8,"J":0.4